text:
erica: Quick follow-up from yesterday's allocator sync — after the async offload patch landed I noticed jitter on long sequences when we cross host pinned -> device boundaries. Can we do a quick design check-in?
sam: saw that too, esp when batch size varies. think fragmentation+sync ordering are the culprits.
vikram: agreed. my hypothesis: the slab allocator's evict path sometimes races with the in-flight kv-cache refcount decrement which causes temporary double-mapped pages and stalls.
lina: repro on my local perf run: 2x P50 increase for sequences > 8k tokens when host staging kicks in. not consistent but correlated with concurrent compaction.
erica: ok tradeoffs: 1) add a lightweight per-slab generation counter and barrier on offload, 2) make compaction cooperative (yielding for outstanding ops), 3) loosen eviction aggressiveness (hysteresis). Thoughts?
vikram: generation counter seems simplest: offload marks slab.genc = ++global_gen; any mapping checks slab.genc == observed_gen. prevents reuse until in-flight ops drain. cost: extra check on hot path.
sam: microbench: generation check is ~3% overhead in our tight loop but eliminates the stall tail. I can add branchless check to reduce cost.
lina: cooperative compaction would reduce host-to-device thrash but increases fragmentation lifetime. Could add a compaction budget per-epoch.
mike: from infra side, hysteresis is safest for production — bump evict_threshold from 0.8 -> 0.9 and add a soft-fallback to smaller model variants if latency spikes. low risk.
erica: can we combine: gen counter + compaction budget + conservative hysteresis? Implementation plan: gen counter for correctness, compaction budget to control latency, hysteresis knobs exposed to runtime config.
vikram: sketch of API change (pseudo):
```
struct Slab { uint64_t gen; /* guard */ ... };
// timeline: caller captures gen, issues async offload, worker validates gen before reusing
bool offload_prepare(Slab *s) {
  uint64_t observed = s->gen;
  // mark as in-flight
  atomic_inc(&s->inflight);
  return observed;
}
void offload_complete(Slab *s, uint64_t observed) {
  atomic_dec(&s->inflight);
  if (s->gen != observed) { /* postpone reuse */ }
}
```
sam: also add metric slab_reuse_postponed_count and slab_inflight_peak. helps us tune budget.
lina: compaction budget: allow X bytes/sec per-NUMA, default 256MB/sec, throttle when p99 latency > target. Should be a runtime flag.
erica: testing plan: unit tests for race, stress with long-seq synthetic workloads, and a dedicated canary on dedicated pool for 48h.
mike: I'll add a canary job and alert for slab_postpone_rate > 0.1% and latency regression > 5%. Deploy-bot: deploy queued for branch allocator-gen-checks on canary cluster.
vikram: one more: alignment for compressed pages. If we reuse slabs that aren't aligned to compression-block boundaries we see decompression overhead. Need to track compression_alignment in slab metadata.
sam: yep add slab.compression_align and ensure alloc returns aligned offset. small memory tax but big perf win for decompression kernel.
erica: action items: sam -> prototype gen-check + metrics (PR by EOD Tue), vikram -> compaction budget sketch + unit tests, lina -> perf harness for long-seq, mike -> canary job + alerts. We'll regroup in 48h.
mike: noted, I've scheduled canary: canary-allocator-gen-193800 on dedicated-3.
erica: thanks all. If anyone hits unexpected regressions ping #eng-oncall and tag me. :thumbsup:
